  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe upper levels this morning features mostly westerly flow \nover southeastern Arizona, while a Pacific low approaches the\nCalifornia coast. Ahead of this low, low amplitude ridging will\nmove over southeastern Arizona today, with a modest increase in\nprecipitable water from the south. This shortwave ridging will\nsuppress overall precipitation chances today, but an isolated\nshower/thunderstorm can't be ruled out with the moisture\nincrease. \n\nWhile the initial Pacific low will cross central Canada into the\nIntermountain West Monday and Tuesday, broad troughing will\nlinger centered over southern California and northern Baja.\nUpstream of this troughing, moisture will surge northward,\nespecially over New Mexico into eastern Arizona. Daily shower\nand thunderstorm chances increase to the 30-50 percent range\nfrom Tuesday through much of the week across Graham, Greenlee,\nand Cochise counties with 10-30 percent chances into Pinal,\nSanta Cruz, and eastern Pima counties.\n\nBy Friday and this weekend, guidance weakens the influence of\nthe upper level trough and precipitation chances come down a\nbit. Chances don't completely fall off however with the\npotential for some tropical influence from the south. There is\nplenty of uncertainty in this scenario at this moment and the\npotential for tropical development off the western Mexico coast\nwill need to be monitored.",
